from ce_common import *
dom('ce-verbal')
topic('Grammar & Agreement')
E('b',"Choose the correct word: Neither of the two books ____ interesting.","is","are","were","have been","Neither is treated as singular when it refers to one of two items, so it takes 'is'.")
E('b',"Choose the correct word: Each of the students ____ a laptop.","has","have","are having","were","'Each' is singular, so the verb is 'has'.")
E('b',"Choose the correct sentence about a third-person subject.","He doesn't know the answer.","He don't know the answer.","He not knows the answer.","He doesn't knows the answer.","With a third-person singular subject, the helping verb is 'doesn't' and the main verb stays in its base form.")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: The number of students ____ increased this year.","has","have","are","were","'The number of' is singular, so it takes 'has'. (Compare 'A number of students are absent', which is plural.)")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: If I ____ you, I would apologise at once.","were","was","am","will be","The unreal conditional uses the subjunctive 'were' for all persons.")
E('a',"Choose the correct word: I suggest that he ____ the form today.","submit","submits","submitted","will submit","After verbs of suggestion or demand, the subjunctive uses the base form of the verb: 'he submit'.")
E('a',"Choose the correct word: Neither the manager nor the employees ____ aware of the change.","were","was","is","has been","With 'neither … nor' the verb agrees with the nearer subject. 'Employees' is plural, so 'were' fits.")
E('a',"Choose the correct pronoun: Between you and ____, the plan will not work.","me","I","myself","mine","After a preposition the object form 'me' is required.")
E('a',"Choose the parallel structure: She likes hiking, swimming and ____.","reading","to read","read","she reads","The items in a list must share the same form. 'Hiking' and 'swimming' are -ing forms, so 'reading' completes the series.")
E('a',"Which sentence is free from a dangling modifier?","Walking down the road, I thought the trees looked beautiful.","Walking down the road, the trees looked beautiful.","Walking down the road, beautiful trees were seen by me looking.","The trees looked beautiful walking down the road.","The modifier 'Walking down the road' must describe the person who walks, so that person ('I') must follow it.")
topic('Tenses & Prepositions')
E('b',"Choose the correct word: She has been working here ____ 2019.","since","for","from","during","'Since' is used with a starting point in time, while 'for' is used with a length of time.")
E('b',"Choose the correct article: He is ____ honest man.","an","a","the","no article","'Honest' begins with a vowel sound because the h is silent, so 'an' is used.")
E('b',"Choose the correct word: The meeting is scheduled ____ Monday.","on","at","in","by","'On' is used before days of the week.")
E('b',"Choose the correct form: I am looking forward to ____ you.","meeting","meet","have met","met","In the phrase 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition and is followed by an -ing form.")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: She is senior ____ me in the company.","to","than","from","over","'Senior' is followed by 'to', not 'than'.")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: He is very good ____ mathematics.","at","in","on","for","The usual collocation is 'good at' a subject or skill.")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: Hardly had he reached the station ____ the train left.","when","than","then","after","'Hardly' is followed by 'when'. ('No sooner' is followed by 'than'.)")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: No sooner did he arrive ____ it began to rain.","than","when","then","but","'No sooner' pairs with 'than'.")
E('i',"Choose the sentence that uses 'despite' correctly.","Despite the rain, we went out.","Despite of the rain, we went out.","Despite the rain, but we went out.","In despite the rain, we went out.","'Despite' is never followed by 'of'. ('In spite of' is the other correct form.)")
topic('Sentence Transformation')
E('i',"Change to passive voice: The committee approved the plan.","The plan was approved by the committee.","The plan is approved by the committee.","The plan had approved the committee.","The committee was approved by the plan.","The object becomes the subject and the verb takes 'was + past participle' to keep the past tense.")
E('i',"Change to reported speech: He said, \"I am tired.\"","He said that he was tired.","He said that I am tired.","He said that he is being tired.","He said me that he was tired.","In reported speech the present tense moves back to the past and the pronoun changes from I to he.")
E('i',"Choose the correctly formed question tag: She can swim, ____?","can't she","isn't she","doesn't she","won't she","A positive statement takes a negative tag that repeats the same auxiliary verb 'can'.")
E('i',"Choose the correct word: The new policy will ____ all employees.","affect","effect","afect","effects","'Affect' is the verb meaning to influence. 'Effect' is normally a noun meaning a result.")
E('i',"Which sentence uses the possessive correctly?","The company announced its results.","The company announced it's results.","The company announced its' results.","The company announced it results.","'Its' is the possessive. 'It's' always means 'it is' or 'it has'.")
E('i',"Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?","I was tired; I went to bed early.","I was tired; because I went to bed early.","I was; tired I went to bed early.","I was tired, I went; to bed early.","A semicolon joins two independent clauses. A clause beginning with 'because' is not independent.")
E('i',"Arrange these sentences in order. P: He opened the door. Q: Someone knocked loudly. R: He walked slowly to the door. S: No one was there.","QRPS","PQRS","QPRS","RQSP","First there is a knock (Q), then he walks to the door (R), opens it (P) and finds no one (S).")
topic('Spelling & Confused Words')
E('b',"Choose the correct spelling of the word for a place to stay.","Accommodation","Accomodation","Acommodation","Accomadation","Accommodation has a double c and a double m.")
E('i',"Choose the correct spelling of the word meaning 'essential'.","Necessary","Neccessary","Necesary","Necessery","Necessary has one c and two s's.")
E('i',"Choose the correct spelling of the word meaning 'an event that happens'.","Occurrence","Occurence","Occurrance","Ocurrence","Occurrence has a double c and a double r.")
E('b',"Choose the correct plural of 'analysis'.","analyses","analysises","analysis","analysi","Words ending in -sis change to -ses in the plural.")
E('b',"Choose the correct plural of 'child'.","children","childs","childrens","childes","'Child' has an irregular plural, 'children'.")
E('i',"Choose the word that fits: She gave her ____ to the proposal after a long discussion.","assent","ascent","accent","ascend","'Assent' means agreement. 'Ascent' means a climb.")
E('b',"What is the past tense of 'lead'?","led","lead","leaded","leed","'Lead' is an irregular verb: lead, led, led.")
E('b',"Which of these words is a noun?","happiness","quickly","beautiful","run","'Happiness' names a feeling, so it is a noun. 'Quickly' is an adverb and 'beautiful' an adjective.")
E('i',"Choose the correct comparative form: This is the ____ of the two books.","better","best","good","more good","When two things are compared the comparative 'better' is used. 'Best' is for three or more.")
topic('Vocabulary')
E('b',"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'rapid'.","swift","slow","steady","careful","'Rapid' and 'swift' both describe something that happens quickly.")
E('b',"Choose the word opposite in meaning to 'ancient'.","modern","old","historic","antique","'Ancient' means very old, so 'modern' is its opposite.")
E('b',"Choose the word opposite in meaning to 'generous'.","stingy","kind","lavish","noble","A stingy person is unwilling to give, the opposite of generous.")
E('b',"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'enormous'.","huge","tiny","ordinary","heavy","'Enormous' means very large, like 'huge'.")
E('i',"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'candid'.","frank","secretive","polite","hesitant","A candid person speaks honestly and openly, which is what 'frank' means.")
E('i',"Choose the word opposite in meaning to 'scarce'.","abundant","rare","limited","costly","'Scarce' means in short supply and 'abundant' means plentiful.")
E('i',"Choose the word opposite in meaning to 'benevolent'.","malevolent","generous","charitable","gracious","'Benevolent' means wishing good, and 'malevolent' means wishing harm.")
E('i',"What does 'ephemeral' mean?","Lasting a very short time","Extremely large","Hidden from view","Easy to break","An ephemeral thing, like a mayfly's life, lasts only a short while.")
E('a',"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'obsequious'.","servile","arrogant","indifferent","sincere","An obsequious person is excessively eager to please and obey, which is servile behaviour.")
E('a',"Choose the word opposite in meaning to 'taciturn'.","talkative","silent","reserved","gloomy","A taciturn person says very little, so 'talkative' is the opposite.")
E('a',"What does 'ubiquitous' mean?","Present everywhere","Rarely seen","Very old","Easily damaged","A ubiquitous thing, such as smartphones, seems to be found everywhere.")
E('a',"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: The judge's ruling was so ____ that nobody could dispute it.","incontrovertible","ambiguous","tentative","arbitrary","'Incontrovertible' means impossible to deny, which fits the idea that no one could dispute it.")
E('a',"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: Her argument, though persuasive, was ____ because it ignored the evidence.","fallacious","cogent","lucid","compelling","'Fallacious' means based on a mistaken idea. The word 'though' signals a contrast with 'persuasive'.")
E('a',"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: Far from being ____, the new manager was surprisingly approachable.","aloof","friendly","helpful","cordial","'Far from being' introduces the opposite of 'approachable'. An aloof person keeps a distance.")
topic('Idioms & One-word Substitution')
E('b',"What does the idiom 'a piece of cake' mean?","Something very easy","Something very sweet","A small gift","A difficult task","The idiom describes a task that needs hardly any effort.")
E('b',"Choose one word for 'government by the people'.","Democracy","Autocracy","Monarchy","Bureaucracy","In a democracy the people hold power, directly or through elected representatives.")
E('i',"What does the idiom 'to bite the bullet' mean?","To face a painful situation bravely","To refuse to listen","To act hastily","To lose your temper","The phrase refers to enduring something unpleasant because it cannot be avoided.")
E('i',"What does the idiom 'once in a blue moon' mean?","Very rarely","Every night","Without warning","In bad weather","A blue moon is a rare event, so the idiom means 'hardly ever'.")
E('i',"Choose one word for 'a person who is present everywhere'.","Omnipresent","Omniscient","Omnipotent","Ominous","'Omnipresent' means present everywhere. 'Omniscient' means all-knowing and 'omnipotent' all-powerful.")
E('a',"What does the idiom 'to throw in the towel' mean?","To give up","To clean up","To start again","To win easily","The phrase comes from boxing, where a towel thrown into the ring signals surrender.")
topic('Reading Comprehension')
E('b',"Passage: Priya wakes at five every day to run before work. She says the quiet streets help her think. Question: Why does Priya run so early?","The quiet streets help her think","She has to reach work early","She dislikes the sun","Her coach tells her to","The passage states it directly: the quiet streets help her think.")
E('i',"Passage: Urban gardens have grown in popularity not merely because they supply fresh vegetables, but because they give city residents a shared purpose. Neighbours who would otherwise never speak end up trading seeds and advice. Question: What does the passage suggest about urban gardens?","They build community as well as provide food","They are cheaper than supermarkets","They are mainly for experts","They have replaced parks","The words 'not merely' show that food is only part of the value. The shared purpose and conversation among neighbours is the rest.")
E('i',"Passage: The company's profits rose sharply last year. However, most of the increase came from a one-time sale of property rather than from selling more products. Question: What can be inferred?","The core business did not necessarily improve","The company sold more products than ever","Profits will keep rising","The property was sold at a loss","The rise came from a one-off sale, so it says little about the ordinary business.")
E('a',"Passage: Critics argue that the subsidy rewards short-term thinking. Supporters reply that without immediate incentives few households would adopt the new technology at all. Question: What is the supporters' main point?","Immediate incentives are needed to get people to adopt the technology","The technology is already popular","Short-term thinking is a good habit","Critics have not studied the subsidy","Supporters answer the criticism by saying adoption would not happen without immediate rewards.")
E('a',"Passage: The minister's statement, hedged with phrases such as 'in some cases' and 'it may be argued', committed him to nothing. Question: What is the author's attitude to the statement?","Critical, suggesting it was evasive","Admiring of its caution","Indifferent to its content","Enthusiastic about its clarity","Saying the statement 'committed him to nothing' shows the author sees it as evasive.")
E('i',"Which word is closest in meaning to 'meticulous'?","careful","careless","quick","modest","A meticulous person pays great attention to detail, which is what 'careful' expresses.")
E('b',"Choose the sentence that uses subject pronouns correctly.","She and I went to the market.","Her and me went to the market.","Me and her went to the market.","She and me went to the market.","The subject form 'she' and 'I' is needed because they are the subject of 'went'.")
E('a',"Choose the sentence with correct subject–verb agreement.","The committee has submitted its report.","The committee have submitted its report.","The committee has submitted their reports.","The committees has submitted its report.","A committee acting as one unit takes a singular verb and the singular pronoun 'its'.")
E('i',"Choose the correct use of 'fewer' or 'less': There are ____ cars on the road today than yesterday.","fewer","less","lesser","least","'Fewer' is used with countable nouns such as cars. 'Less' is used with uncountable ones.")
